{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"1fd4ae39-09c4-4cf0-9bfe-fc8abaa281eb","hash":"a4d91d2672ca523b1a7352d8208361c7a36677db0a4104b030ae59c72e2ef23d","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01H2xwyH7N3WALKzhoJyni2c","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"OneXray fork feasibility study","subagent_type":"general-purpose","prompt":"You are researching for a small Russian VPN service \"Nexo\" (owner is non-technical; one AI developer). Context you must take as given:\n- Servers run Xray (3x-ui) with VLESS over TLS and VLESS Reality (Vienna + Brazil). Clients are in Russia, mostly iPhone and Android.\n- Our subscription server already serves: a base64 list of vless:// links, and an Xray JSON array (full configs with our routing rules: Russian banks/Gosuslugi direct, everything else via proxy).\n- Today iPhone users use OneXray (recommended), Karing, or Happ. Owner likes OneXray's stability but wants an app of our own: ONE simple screen (big On/Off button, status, server choice, \"support\"), branded \"Nexo\", key arrives via a deep link from our Telegram bot. He finds Karing too complex.\n- Owner's Mac has only ~6.6 GB free disk, no Flutter/Xcode toolchain; builds would have to run on GitHub Actions. No Apple developer account yet. GitHub account exists.\n\nYour task: a feasibility study of FORKING OneXray (github.com/YuanDevTeam/OneXray, GPL-3, Flutter + libXray, iOS/macOS/Android/Windows/Linux, App Store id6745748773, by Yuan Dev LLC, site onexray.com) into a stripped, rebranded, one-screen app.\nUse `gh api` / `gh repo view` to inspect the repo (read-only), the docs/ folder, .github/workflows, pubspec.yaml, ios/ and android/ directories, lib/pages, lib/service. Also read onexray.com docs. Do NOT contact anyone, do NOT fork, do NOT create anything on GitHub.\n\nFind out and report:\n1. Code shape: size (files/LOC), how UI pages are organized vs the core service layer; how hard it is to replace the UI with one screen while keeping the connect/subscription/tunnel services. Point to concrete directories/files.\n2. Build: does the repo have GitHub Actions workflows that build Android APK and iOS? What do they require (secrets, signing, macOS runners, libXray binaries prebuilt or built from source, Go version)? Is libXray vendored/prebuilt? Estimated CI minutes/cost for iOS builds on GitHub-hosted macOS runners.\n3. iOS specifics: Network Extension target, entitlements, App Groups, memory handling for the 50 MB NE limit — how does OneXray deal with it (search for memory/limit/GOGC/debug.SetMemoryLimit etc.).\n4. Deep link / subscription import that we already use: onexray://onexray.com/sub/add?url=...&mode=providerManaged — where it is handled; could a fork keep this so our bot's existing buttons work with a custom scheme (e.g. nexo://)?\n5. License & naming: GPL-3 obligations for a rebranded fork sold commercially; any trademark/name restrictions in the repo (LICENSE, README, NOTICE); the known GPL-vs-App-Store tension and how projects handle it (author permission/additional permission). Is there a single copyright holder (Yuan Dev LLC) — i.e. could one permission/commercial license solve it? Any contact channel listed (don't contact).\n6. Stability reputation: App Store ratings, GitHub issues count/themes (crashes? reconnects? battery?), release cadence.\n7. Your estimate: effort in developer-weeks for (a) Android-only one-screen fork with APK distribution, (b) iOS added; main risks; and whether forking OneXray is better or worse than writing a minimal app from scratch on libXray. Give confidence levels.\n\nReport in Russian, ≤ 900 words, structured with headings, concrete file paths and source URLs. State clearly what you verified vs inferred."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PmajfbETburqw5wxQBd7ec","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Compare open-source VPN client bases","subagent_type":"general-purpose","prompt":"You are researching for a small Russian VPN service \"Nexo\" (non-technical owner, one AI developer). Given facts:\n- Servers: Xray (3x-ui), VLESS+TLS and VLESS Reality. Clients in Russia on iPhone and Android. Subscription server serves base64 vless:// list and Xray JSON full configs with routing rules.\n- Goal: our OWN branded app \"Nexo\": ONE simple screen (big On/Off, status, server choice, support), key via deep link from our Telegram bot; must be stable; iPhone matters most (Happ is absent from the Russian App Store; Karing and OneXray are present). Builds would run on GitHub Actions (owner's Mac is nearly full). No Apple developer account yet.\n- Already known (don't re-derive, but correct if wrong): Hiddify uses an \"extended GPL\" that forbids commercial use/renaming; Karing (KaringX/karing) is GPL-3 Flutter with modified sing-box core; v2rayNG and FlClash are GPL-3; Xray-core is MPL-2.0; sing-box is GPL with a clause that derivative work may not use the name; OneXray is GPL-3 Flutter + libXray (studied separately — skip it).\n\nTask: compare OTHER candidate open-source bases for a rebranded one-screen Xray/sing-box client on iOS+Android. Candidates to check (add others you find): Karing, Hiddify, FlClash, sing-box official apps (SFI/SFA, github.com/SagerNet/sing-box-for-apple, sing-box-for-android), v2rayNG (Android), \"Clash Mi\" (KaringX), \"sing-box MT\", AmneziaVPN (github.com/amnezia-vpn/amnezia-client, supports Xray?), Outline client / Outline SDK (Jigsaw; which protocols; Reality?), any Flutter/React Native \"Xray client template\" repos, \"V2rayU\", \"Mihomo party\", \"Throne\"/NekoBox, Shadowrocket-like closed ones are out of scope.\nFor each: license and whether rebrand + commercial sale + App Store publishing is allowed; iOS + Android support; VLESS Reality support; code size/complexity and how separable UI is from core; build system/CI presence; maintenance activity (last release, open issues, stars); known stability problems; iOS Network Extension memory handling.\nUse `gh api`/`gh repo view` (read-only), WebFetch, WebSearch. Do not fork/create anything.\n\nDeliver in Russian, ≤ 900 words: a comparison table (base | license OK for rebrand+commercial? | iOS | Android | Reality | UI separable | CI | activity | stability notes), then a ranked shortlist of the top 3 bases with reasons, and explicit \"do not use\" list with reasons. Mark verified vs inferred; include source URLs."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019WR9irFW9LUTyTBTGHHZsT","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"From-scratch minimal VPN app architecture","subagent_type":"general-purpose","prompt":"You are a senior mobile/network engineer advising a small Russian VPN service \"Nexo\" (non-technical owner, one AI developer, builds on GitHub Actions, no Apple dev account yet). Servers: Xray (3x-ui) with VLESS+TLS and VLESS Reality; subscription server serves base64 vless:// list and full Xray JSON configs with routing rules. Goal: an own-branded app with ONE simple screen (big On/Off button, status, server pick, support), key via deep link (nexo://...) from a Telegram bot. iPhone is the priority (Russian App Store), Android second.\n\nTask: design and cost a MINIMAL app written from scratch (not a fork), and surface the hidden pitfalls. Research with WebSearch/WebFetch and `gh api` (read-only) the current state (as of late 2026) of:\n1. Core options: libXray (github.com/XTLS/libXray — license, gomobile bindings for iOS .xcframework and Android .aar, API surface, who uses it), Xray-core via gomobile directly, sing-box as library (libbox) and its license clause; which is simplest for VLESS Reality + routing.\n2. iOS: NetworkExtension PacketTunnelProvider; tun2socks choices (hev-socks5-tunnel, go tun2socks, sing-tun) and how Xray's TUN inbound / \"tun\" on iOS works; the 50 MB memory limit of the NE process — concrete mitigations used by Happ/OneXray/Karing/FoXray (GOGC, debug.SetMemoryLimit, disabling stats, DNS caching, Go runtime flags); App Groups to pass config; \"Always-on/On-demand\"; what Apple requires for VPN apps (Guideline 5.4, entitlement com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension, needs paid developer account; is a company account needed for VPN apps?).\n3. Android: VpnService + tun2socks or Xray TUN; battery/Doze; Android 14/15 specifics; APK distribution (signing, R8), RuStore/Google Play notes briefly.\n4. Framework: Flutter vs React Native vs native SwiftUI + Kotlin for a one-screen app with a Go core: which minimizes risk and build complexity on GitHub Actions; CI cost (macOS runners ~$0.08/min; estimate minutes for an iOS build), code signing on CI (fastlane match, API keys).\n5. Realistic plan: milestones, developer-weeks for Android MVP, iOS MVP, polishing; ongoing maintenance load (Xray updates, iOS releases); typical bugs that make such apps \"glitchy\" (reconnect after network change, sleep/wake, DNS leaks, killed NE process) and how mature apps solve them.\n6. Verdict: from-scratch vs fork of an existing Flutter+libXray app — which gives a stable app sooner for a one-person team.\n\nReport in Russian, ≤ 1000 words, with headings, concrete numbers and source URLs; mark verified vs estimated."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011cL7qotzypJCaPY5jQBcZS","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"App distribution and legal risks","subagent_type":"general-purpose","prompt":"You are researching distribution and legal risk for a small Russian VPN service \"Nexo\" that is considering publishing its OWN branded VPN client app (iOS + Android) for clients in Russia. Owner is non-technical, Russian-speaking. Today clients use third-party apps (OneXray, Karing, Happ) with a subscription link from a Telegram bot. Do not contact anyone; research only (WebSearch extended mode where facts are recent, WebFetch, official sources).\n\nFind out, as of late 2026, with dates and sources:\n1. Apple App Store in Russia: history and current state of VPN app removals at Roskomnadzor's request (2024–2026): how many removed, examples, whether removals target apps that advertise \"bypass\" vs neutral \"proxy utility\" apps; which Xray clients remain in the RU store (Karing, OneXray, v2RAGE, Npv Tunnel...) and what they have in common (wording, no built-in servers?). Practical rules for app wording/description to survive review and RKN.\n2. Apple developer account: individual vs organization (D-U-N-S), cost, whether VPN apps (NetworkExtension entitlement) require an organization account; can a Russian citizen/company currently open and pay for an Apple developer account (payment restrictions), common workarounds (foreign entity); App Review Guideline 5.4 VPN requirements; TestFlight as distribution (limits: 10,000 testers, 90-day builds, review needed) and whether it's used by VPN services in RU as a workaround.\n3. Android: Google Play VPN policy (VpnService declaration), RuStore availability for VPN apps (does RuStore accept VPN/proxy clients? examples), direct APK distribution risks (users' \"unknown sources\"), Huawei AppGallery.\n4. Russian law: 2025–2026 rules on VPN \"popularization\"/advertising fines, blocking of VPN protocols by TSPU (which protocols are blocked/throttled; status of VLESS Reality), any obligations for a VPN service operator/app publisher (registration in RKN registry, \"white lists\"), liability risk for the owner of a branded app vs. a bot that just hands out keys to third-party apps. Keep it factual, cite sources (RKN, laws, reputable media).\n5. Open-source license vs stores: GPL-3 code on the App Store — the known conflict and how projects (VLC, Karing, OneXray) handle it; what a rebranded GPL fork must publish; whether a closed app can use Xray-core (MPL-2.0) and libXray.\n6. Summary: a risk matrix (risk | likelihood | impact | mitigation) for: own iOS app, own Android app, staying on third-party apps + web cabinet.\n\nReport in Russian, ≤ 1000 words, headings, sources with dates. Mark what is confirmed vs uncertain."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01N7HdwDYFPZ6oRRhvV5xxmp","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"White-label branded VPN app options","subagent_type":"general-purpose","prompt":"You are researching \"buy or rent instead of build\" options for a small Russian VPN service \"Nexo\" (Xray/VLESS Reality servers via 3x-ui, subscription link from a Telegram bot, clients in Russia on iPhone/Android). The owner wants clients to see a simple, stable app under the Nexo brand, or at least a seamless branded experience, without building a full app himself. Research only, do not contact vendors.\n\nInvestigate, as of late 2026, with prices/terms and source URLs:\n1. Branding features inside existing clients that providers can use via subscription headers/deep links: Happ (routing profiles, \"announce\", custom name/logo, \"Happ Lite\" for RU store?), v2RayTun (provider branding program), Streisand, Karing ISP cooperation (karing.app/blog/isp/cooperation: what exactly a provider gets — login=subscription, recommendation listing, custom branding?), OneXray (subscription-userinfo, profile-title, isp headers), Hiddify profile headers. Which of these are available in the RUSSIAN App Store today.\n2. White-label / turnkey branded VPN apps for Xray/VLESS providers: vendors that sell \"your own app with your logo\" (search in Russian and English: \"white label VPN приложение xray\", \"брендированное приложение VPN для панели\", Remnawave ecosystem apps, Marzban ecosystem, \"PasarGuard\", \"Hiddify white label\", \"VPN app builder\", telegram channels/forums like searchengines/habr/4pda; GitHub projects like \"remnawave-subscription-page\", \"Happ branding\"). For each: what you get (iOS? Android? store publishing under whose account?), price model (one-time/monthly/per-user), dependency/lock-in risks, reputation.\n3. Half-way option: a branded web \"subscription page\"/mini-app (Remnawave subscription page, Marzban sub page templates, Telegram Mini App) that shows the Nexo brand and one-tap \"add to app\" buttons for OneXray/Karing/Happ — examples, repos, effort.\n4. Apple \"Custom App\"/Business Manager or Unlisted App distribution as a way to publish a branded app with less exposure — is it applicable to VPN clients?\n5. Compare: (a) stay on third-party apps + branded sub page, (b) white-label vendor app, (c) own fork — in a table: time to launch, cost, control, risk of disappearance from stores, UX simplicity for a non-technical user.\n\nReport in Russian, ≤ 900 words, headings, table, sources with dates; mark verified vs unverified claims."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01EPpjXpQhcUGWnDSYTpSaHt","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"One-screen VPN app UX spec","subagent_type":"general-purpose","prompt":"You are a product/UX researcher. A small Russian VPN service \"Nexo\" wants its own client app that non-technical Russian users find effortless: owner's words — stable like OneXray, but not complex like Karing. Flow today: user taps \"Add key to <app>\" in a Telegram bot → the app opens via deep link and imports a subscription (servers in Vienna and Brazil, Russian banks/Gosuslugi routed directly automatically) → user turns VPN on. Tariffs: 7-day trial, 1/3/12 months, up to 3 devices. Compliance constraint: the app must not use words about \"bypassing blocks\"; neutral wording (\"защищённое соединение\", \"прокси-утилита\").\n\nTask: produce a concrete UX specification for a ONE-screen app, grounded in how the best simple VPN apps do it. Research (WebSearch/WebFetch, App Store/Google Play pages, screenshots descriptions, reviews) the UI patterns of: OneXray, Happ, Karing (incl. its \"beginner mode\"), AmneziaVPN, v2RAGE, Streisand, plus mainstream consumer apps (NordVPN, Proton VPN, Cloudflare WARP 1.1.1.1 — the gold standard of one-button simplicity). Read Russian-language user reviews/complaints about OneXray, Karing, Happ (App Store RU, 4pda, Habr, Telegram) to list the most common confusions and bugs users hit (\"не подключается\", \"нет кнопки\", \"правила\", \"подписка не обновляется\").\n\nDeliver in Russian, ≤ 1000 words:\n1. Top-10 user confusions/complaints with these apps (with sources).\n2. The Nexo one-screen spec: layout (what is on screen, exact Russian copy for every label), states (no key / key added / connecting / connected / error / subscription expired / no internet), the deep-link onboarding flow step by step (what the user sees from the bot tap to \"connected\", including iOS \"Allow VPN configuration\" prompt), server choice (auto vs Austria/Brazil), \"Помощь\" and \"Поддержка\" entry points, how expiry/renewal is shown without payment inside the app (link back to the bot), update of subscription in background.\n3. What to deliberately leave OUT (settings, logs, routing editors) and the one hidden \"advanced\" escape hatch.\n4. Accessibility/compliance notes: wording that passes App Store review and avoids RU \"VPN popularization\" risk.\nInclude source URLs; mark what is observed vs your design proposal."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
