{"parentUuid":"46a9e7f2-b0dc-4c7e-9610-8845ab5fc1e9","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"deferred_tools_record","entries":[{"name":"mcp__terminal__list_terminal_tabs","description":"List the open tabs of the user's Terminal panel for this session: tab_id, title, the directory each shell started in, and whether a command is currently running in it (null when that cannot be determined). Runs nothing.","input_schema":{"type":"object","properties":{},"$schema":"http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#"},"eager_input_streaming":true,"defer_loading":true},{"name":"mcp__terminal__run_in_terminal","description":"Type a shell command into a tab of the user's Terminal panel (beside this conversation) and press Enter. The command runs in the user's interactive login shell (zsh, bash or sh, with their aliases, PATH and credentials) — on their computer, or on the SSH host in an SSH session — outside any sandbox, stays running after your turn ends, and its tab is added without opening the panel: the user can open the panel to watch it, type into it and stop it with Ctrl-C; you stop it, or close its tab when you are done with it, with stop_terminal_tab. Returns the tab's tab_id once the command is typed, which waits for the shell's startup files to finish (usually at once, longer for a slow shell); read what it printed with read_terminal {tab_id, wait_for_output_ms}. Prefer this over Bash for long-running or interactive things the user should see and control — dev servers, file watchers, test runners in watch mode, log tails, REPLs — and over preview_start for processes that are not an HTTP server to preview. Use Bash for short commands whose output you need in this turn. With no tab_id a call opens a new tab in cwd (default the session's working directory). Do not open a tab per command: to run the next command, pass the tab_id of a tab you opened earlier whose shell is back at its prompt (list_terminal_tabs marks it reusable: true), and keep new tabs for things that keep running, like a dev server or a watcher. A reused tab is the same shell as before, so its directory, aliases and variables are wherever earlier commands left them, the output of earlier commands stays above the new output, and cwd cannot be combined with tab_id. Give each tab you open its own short title of letters, digits, spaces and . _ , & + : # / ' -: the approval card names the tab, so one whose name is missing, uses other characters or reads the same as another tab's cannot be reused, and one renamed before the user answers is refused (ask again with the names list_terminal_tabs shows). `command` is one complete command line in printable ASCII, as you would type it at the prompt: pipelines, && / || / ;, quotes, variables, redirections and subshells are all fine. Refused before anything is typed: more than one line, control or non-ASCII characters, a line the prompt would wait on (trailing \\ or operator, a << here-document, an unclosed quote or parenthesis, exec with an input redirection, an unfinished if/for/while), PROMPT_COMMAND, HISTFILE, histchars or zsh's STTY, module_path, fpath, KEYBOARD_HACK or ZDOTDIR named anywhere ($NAME reads pass, and so does a NAME= with no value at the start of the line unless the line also holds an empty $( )), trap actions, a prompt, mail-check or zsh arithmetic parameter (PS1, PROMPT, RPROMPT, MAILPATH, TMOUT, HISTSIZE ...; SECONDS, RANDOM and the like only when assigned a computed value, COLUMNS, LINES, UID and the like only on a line with a [) assigned on a line with any $ expansion or backtick, or named on a line with a $(, ${, $'...', a lone unquoted $, a backtick or numeric escape or one that reads input, the shell's hook and handler functions (precmd, zshexit, TRAPINT, command_not_found_handler, vcs_info, __git_ps1, git_prompt_info, vcs_info's +vi-... ..., or a name containing a zsh hook's such as _p9k_precmd) on a line that defines, copies or autoloads a function or as an alias name, a read-family builtin with any $ operand, a printf -v, declare-style setter (or zsh's zpty -r / set -A) whose target name holds a $, a nameref declared beside any $ or to one of those parameters, zsh's zle-line-init-style hook widgets, a zstyle ... hooks or zstyle -e setting, and sched jobs (a name split by a $(...), backtick pair or unset ${x} still counts), print -P with a $ or glob, `!` history expansions (sudo !!, a ! inside a word), $_ anywhere the shell would expand it, the history commands fc, r, history, pushln, and print with any later -word containing s, S or z (quoted text included), zsh glob qualifiers that run code (*(e:...:), *(+cmd)) and parentheses shaped like them, zsh's ${(flags)x} / $~x / $=x / ${a:-${b}} forms, a : right after a bare $name (write ${x:t}, or ${x}:text), $'...' numeric escapes, any $'...' escape, numeric backslash escape or glued {a,b} list beside eval, source, ssh, tmux, <<< or another shell, and words starting with # — and anything the user's shell cannot parse, reported with the shell's own error. Put longer scripts in a file (Write) and run the file here. In default mode the user approves the exact line; in auto mode the classifier judges it as it would the same command run through Bash with the sandbox disabled, read as typed at an interactive prompt (in an SSH session the user is always asked, whatever the mode). In an SSH session the command is typed only when the host confirms nothing else is running in the new shell. A command that could not be typed is reported with the reason and what became of the tab.","input_schema":{"type":"object","properties":{"command":{"description":"The command line to type, exactly as the user will see it approved and run. One line.","type":"string"},"tab_id":{"description":"A tab you opened earlier (the tab_id from run_in_terminal, open_terminal_tab or list_terminal_tabs) to run the command in instead of opening a new one; list_terminal_tabs marks the tabs that can take it with reusable: true (one you opened, at its prompt with nothing running, in which the user has typed nothing, with a name of its own). Cannot be combined with cwd; a title is not used (the approval card names the tab by its own name).","type":"string"},"cwd":{"description":"Directory to start the new tab's shell in: absolute, ~, or relative to the session's working directory; must be inside a directory this session has access to. Default: the session's working directory.","type":"string"},"title":{"description":"Short label for the tab, e.g. \"dev server\".","type":"string"},"_consent":{"description":"Set by the app. Do not set it.","type":"string"}},"required":["command"],"$schema":"http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#"},"eager_input_streaming":true,"defer_loading":true}]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"253b23d3-0279-4dfe-bd4d-df0a45ba0c38","timestamp":"2026-11-02T12:51:57.871Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/Sagenkazaran/Library/Application Support/Claude/scratch-workspaces/f907b165-4712-485f-a44b-7f7d54b74cf9/77d6453f-aaee-4ad3-a7ea-34b17cbaf2e5/scratch-2026-10-26-b13584","sessionId":"1fd4ae39-09c4-4cf0-9bfe-fc8abaa281eb","version":"2.1.309","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"deep-singing-taco"}
